{"vid":"JzBz0fjhhQg","source":"JzBz0fjhhQg.en-GB.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":0,"txt":"Stop pronouncing this letter in British English. Okay, I don't mean all the time,"},{"t":9.2,"txt":"but in so many words the 'g' is silent. You don't need to pronounce it and if you're thinking why is"},{"t":17.7,"txt":"the 'g' even there if we don't need to pronounce it, you are not alone. Here's a fun fact:"},{"t":25,"txt":"every single letter in the alphabet is silent in at least one word in English."},{"t":32.7,"txt":"If you want a video on that, let me know and I will make it for you. I think you"},{"t":37.6,"txt":"might be surprised by just how many words contain a silent 'g'. In today's lesson,"},{"t":45.2,"txt":"we will go through some common letter combinations that will help you to identify the silent 'g,'"},{"t":51.8,"txt":"so you don't have to just memorise hundreds of words."},{"t":55.5,"txt":"Instead, you can recognise where it is likely to be silent. Now, I know you love shadowing, so"},{"t":62,"txt":"I've included several shadowing tasks throughout the video to improve your accuracy and increase"},{"t":68.9,"txt":"your fluency. Remember that with shadowing you have 3 options: you can try and speak with me,"},{"t":76.2,"txt":"you can try to speak just after me—I don't know why I'm doing that so much, I'm enjoying it—or"},{"t":82.8,"txt":"you can pause the video and listen and repeat. All 3 will help you with your pronunciation."},{"t":90,"txt":"So, the silent 'g' might seem like a small detail, but mastering little things like this"},{"t":98,"txt":"is what is going to make your English sound truly natural and truly effortless. If you'd like to"},{"t":104.4,"txt":"take your British English pronunciation even further, join my 5-Day British Accent Bootcamp."},{"t":112.5,"txt":"It is 5 days of focused, practical lessons covering the sounds,"},{"t":118.7,"txt":"words and habits that make you sound instantly more British,"},{"t":123.6,"txt":"including the 'r' and 't' sounds and the subtle but notoriously difficult differences in vowel"},{"t":131.3,"txt":"sounds. My lovely student Bob said that the Bootcamp helped him understand the sounds"},{"t":137.2,"txt":"and pronounce them from day one. At the moment, I'm offering lifetime access and a huge discount,"},{"t":145.3,"txt":"so why not start today and see how much your pronunciation can improve in just 5 days?"},{"t":151.6,"txt":"To learn more and join, click on the link in the description box or scan that QR code there. Okay,"},{"t":158.1,"txt":"let's start with the letters '-gn'. Never pronounce the letter 'g' in this letter"},{"t":164.7,"txt":"combination at the beginning of words like 'gnat'. By the way, you should be repeating after me here."},{"t":173.8,"txt":"'Gnash,' 'gnarled,' 'gnaw,' 'gnome,' 'gnocchi' and 'gnu'."},{"t":191.6,"txt":"So it's not /ɡnɔː/ /ɡnəʊm/ or /ˈɡənɒki/, it's just 'gnaw',"},{"t":198.2,"txt":"'gnome,' 'gnocchi.' And 'gnocchi' has an invisible /j/ sound just before the /ɒ/."},{"t":205.8,"txt":"Some people drop it and say NOCK-ee. 'Gnu' also has an optional /j/ sound. So, nyoo or noo?"},{"t":215,"txt":"And what's a 'gnu,' I hear you cry? It's a large African animal with horns that eats grass."},{"t":221.6,"txt":"They are also called 'wildebeest'. Now, I know this lesson is called never pronounce"},{"t":227.3,"txt":"the letter 'g' in British English, but I have to let you know about an alternative pronunciation"},{"t":233.9,"txt":"for 'gnu' or /njuː/. It can also be pronounced:"},{"t":238.6,"txt":"/ɡəˈnuː/. Let's practise the silent 'g' in these words. Shadow me now or listen, pause and"},{"t":245.8,"txt":"then repeat. Remember that the 'g' is completely silent and the 'n' or /n/ is pronounced normally."},{"t":254.1,"txt":"Ready? 'The gnats gnawed at my ankles as I ate my gnocchi.'"},{"t":263.9,"txt":"'The gnu sat under the gnarled tree, gnashing its teeth.'"},{"t":272.3,"txt":"Okay, we also never pronounce the 'g' when we have the letters '-gn'"},{"t":278.4,"txt":"at the end of a word or near the end of a word. For example, repeat after me: 'Reign,'"},{"t":288.6,"txt":"'foreign,' 'campaign,' 'champagne,'"},{"t":299.1,"txt":"'cologne,' 'sovereign' and 'consign'. The letter 'g' stays silent, even if we change the word"},{"t":312.6,"txt":"by adding letters to the end. For example, and repeat after me: 'foreign - foreigner,'"},{"t":323.6,"txt":"'campaign - campaigning,' 'sovereign - sovereignty,' 'consign - consignment'."},{"t":337.6,"txt":"The letter 'g' stays silent. The letter 'g' is also never pronounced in these words: 'sign,'"},{"t":348.1,"txt":"'assign,' 'design' and 'resign'. But we need to be a little more careful here,"},{"t":361,"txt":"as the sneaky little 'g' sound reappears with some suffixes. For example, repeat after me:"},{"t":370.2,"txt":"'sign,' 'signature,' 'signal,' 'assign,' 'assignation,' 'design,'"},{"t":382,"txt":"'designate,' 'designation' and 'resign,' 'resignation'."},{"t":391,"txt":"Did you notice how the vowel sound changed in those words too,"},{"t":394.9,"txt":"the /aɪ/ diphthong in 'sign' and 'assign' switched to a short /ɪ/: 'signal,'"},{"t":404,"txt":"'signature,' 'assignation.' The /ɪ/ vowel sound in 'design' and 'resign' switches to /e/:"},{"t":414.9,"txt":"'designate,' 'designation,' 'resignation'. Let's practise,"},{"t":422.7,"txt":"I've marked the silent '-g's' in red and the pronounced '-g's' in green. Shadow me if you can."},{"t":431.7,"txt":"'The designated heir to the throne resigned to open a champagne and cologne business.'"},{"t":443.1,"txt":"Never pronounce the letter 'g' when it appears with 'n' in the middle of the words:"},{"t":449.9,"txt":"'bolognese,' 'lasagne,' 'vignette,' and 'poignant'. This is the English pronunciation,"},{"t":463.2,"txt":"not the Italian and French pronunciation. These words have formed a part of the English language"},{"t":469.2,"txt":"now. Let's move on to another letter combination now: '-gh'. Never pronounce the 'g' at the end or"},{"t":477.5,"txt":"near the end of words like 'high' and 'height', 'sigh' and 'sight', 'weigh' and 'weight'."},{"t":488.8,"txt":"So, 'height' and 'sight' both have the /aɪ/ vowel sound. Can you think of any"},{"t":495.8,"txt":"other words with that /aɪ/ sound that have '-gh' at or near the end? Well,"},{"t":503.1,"txt":"we have loads and they all have a silent 'g'. For example: 'bright,' 'flight,' tight,' 'delight'"},{"t":519.9,"txt":"and 'fortnight'. Never pronounce the 'g' in these words. The letters '-gh' also appear a lot in"},{"t":530.3,"txt":"words with the /eɪ/ vowel sound like we saw with 'weigh' and 'weight', can you think of any more?"},{"t":539.6,"txt":"Well, there is 'eight,' 'freight,'"},{"t":545.6,"txt":"'sleigh' and 'neighbour,' to name a few. Shall we practise? Shadow me now."},{"t":554.6,"txt":"'I sighed when I realised the height and weight of my case were too much for freight.'"},{"t":565.7,"txt":"And: 'We saw eight bright stars at night, much to my neighbour's delight.'"},{"t":576.9,"txt":"And finally, never pronounce the 'g' in letter combinations with '-augh' and '-ough'. For example:"},{"t":588.7,"txt":"'dough', 'doughnut' and 'furlough'. These all have the vowel sound /əʊ/. We then have 'plough'"},{"t":603,"txt":"and 'drought' with the /aʊ/ sound, and then we have words like 'ought,' 'fraught,' 'naughty' and"},{"t":619.9,"txt":"'daughter'. All with the /ɔː/ vowel sound. Don't pronounce the letter 'g' in any of these words."},{"t":628.7,"txt":"Let's practise the silent 'g' now. Shadow me."},{"t":633.3,"txt":"'I'm fraught because your naughty daughter ploughed her way through all the doughnuts.'"},{"t":644,"txt":"Now, sometimes the letter 'g' isn't technically silent, but combines with 'h' to make a /f/"},{"t":653.6,"txt":"sound. Can you think of any words in which the letters 'gh' are pronounced /f/? Well,"},{"t":661.2,"txt":"we have some at the end of '-ough' words like 'rough', 'tough', 'cough' and 'enough'. We also"},{"t":675.7,"txt":"have some with '-augh' like 'draught' and 'laugh'. Now, be careful, we do pronounce the 'g' in 'gh'"},{"t":690,"txt":"at the beginning of words like 'ghost', 'ghastly',"},{"t":697.7,"txt":"'ghetto' and 'gherkin', it's actually the 'h' that is silent in all of those words. I told you there"},{"t":708,"txt":"are silent letters all over the place in English words. Our final letter combination is '-gm'."},{"t":716.1,"txt":"English has a few Greek words ending in '-gm' which have a silent 'g'. Can you think of any?"},{"t":725.3,"txt":"Well, the words 'phlegm',"},{"t":728.9,"txt":"'paradigm' and 'diaphragm' all have a silent 'g'. And that, my lovely students,"},{"t":738.3,"txt":"brings us to the end of today's pronunciation lesson. Were there any words that surprised you?"},{"t":744.5,"txt":"I wonder which of the words we looked at today do you find particularly challenging to pronounce?"},{"t":751.4,"txt":"Let me know in the comments. Also, don't forget about my 5-Day British Accent Bootcamp."},{"t":758,"txt":"I'm offering lifetime access and a huge discount. To learn more, click on the"},{"t":763.2,"txt":"link in the description box or scan that QR code there. I will see you in the next lesson! Muah!"}],"words":[{"t":0,"w":"Stop"},{"t":0,"w":"pronouncing"},{"t":0,"w":"this"},{"t":0,"w":"letter"},{"t":0,"w":"in"},{"t":0,"w":"British"},{"t":0,"w":"English."},{"t":0,"w":"Okay,"},{"t":0,"w":"I"},{"t":0,"w":"don't"},{"t":0,"w":"mean"},{"t":0,"w":"all"},{"t":0,"w":"the"},{"t":0,"w":"time,"},{"t":9.24,"w":"but"},{"t":9.24,"w":"in"},{"t":9.24,"w":"so"},{"t":9.24,"w":"many"},{"t":9.24,"w":"words"},{"t":9.24,"w":"the"},{"t":9.24,"w":"'g'"},{"t":9.24,"w":"is"},{"t":9.24,"w":"silent."},{"t":9.24,"w":"You"},{"t":9.24,"w":"don't"},{"t":9.24,"w":"need"},{"t":9.24,"w":"to"},{"t":9.24,"w":"pronounce"},{"t":9.24,"w":"it"},{"t":9.24,"w":"and"},{"t":9.24,"w":"if"},{"t":9.24,"w":"you're"},{"t":9.24,"w":"thinking"},{"t":9.24,"w":"why"},{"t":9.24,"w":"is"},{"t":17.72,"w":"the"},{"t":17.72,"w":"'g'"},{"t":17.7